      "whyThisWorks": "Slow braising extracts collagen from chuck, producing a silky broth enriched with toasted chiles for deep, complex flavour. The tare concentrates umami (tamari, fish sauce) and balances acidity (vinegar) to season the broth just before serving. Sous-vide eggs cooked at 63 °C achieve custardy yolks that meld into the soup, echoing the richness of traditional ramen. Compliance refinement: the revised tare uses low-sodium tamari, less fish sauce, unsalted stock, and non-alcoholic acidity by default.",
      "plating": "Serve in deep bowls with long-handled chopsticks and spoons. The broth should be glossy with a gentle chile sheen. Pair with a crisp Kölsch beer (limit to one glass) or a chilled hibiscus-lime agua fresca.",
      "fullMarkdown": "## Birria Ramen with Sous-Vide Egg (Mexican × Japanese)\n**Servings: 4 | Active time: 1 h | Total time: 6 h (includes braising)**\n\n**Mise en place**\n- Station A - Birria broth: Toast 5 dried guajillo and 3 ancho chiles until fragrant; rehydrate in hot water. Sear 800 g lamb or beef chuck in a Dutch oven; gather aromatics (1 onion, 4 garlic cloves, 2 bay leaves, 5 g cumin seeds, 5 g coriander seeds).\n- Station B - Ramen tare: Combine 60 ml tamari, 60 ml unsalted mushroom stock; sake optional for adults, 30 ml mirin, 30 ml fish sauce and 30 ml rice vinegar.\n- Station C - Noodles & toppings: Prepare 800 g fresh whole-wheat ramen noodles; slice 200 g shiitake mushrooms, 100 g radishes, chop 30 g cilantro, slice 2 limes. Set up sous-vide water bath at 63 °C for eggs.\n\n**Ingredients**\n- Birria: 800 g grass-fed lamb or beef chuck cut into chunks, 5 dried guajillo chiles, 3 dried ancho chiles, 1 onion, 4 garlic cloves, 1 stick cinnamon, 2 bay leaves, 5 g each cumin and coriander seeds, 500 ml beef stock, 500 ml water.\n- Ramen: 800 g fresh whole-wheat ramen noodles, 4 eggs (for sous-vide), 200 g shiitake mushrooms, 100 g radishes, 100 g bean sprouts, 30 g cilantro, lime wedges.\n- Tare: 45 ml low-sodium tamari, 60 ml unsalted mushroom stock or 30 ml rice vinegar + 30 ml water; sake only as an optional adult variation, 30 ml mirin or apple-cider vinegar, 15 ml fish sauce, 30 ml rice vinegar.\n- Garnish: 20 g toasted sesame seeds, 2 spring onions sliced.\n\n**Method**\n1. Make birria: Toast chiles briefly in a dry pan, then soak in 250 ml boiling water until soft. Blend with soaking liquid, onion, garlic, spices and a pinch of salt to a smooth paste. Sear meat in a Dutch oven until browned; add chile purée, cinnamon stick, bay leaves, stock and water. Bring to a simmer, cover and braise in a 150 °C oven for 4-5 hours until meat is tender. Shred meat and return to broth; adjust salt.\n2. Prepare eggs: Submerge eggs in a 63 °C sous-vide bath for 45 min (for jammy yolks). Shock in ice water, then carefully crack and slide into bowls later.\n3. Cook noodles & toppings: Sauté mushrooms in a drop of sesame oil until browned; season lightly. Blanch ramen noodles in boiling water for 1 min (fresh) or until al dente; rinse briefly to stop cooking.\n4. Assemble bowls: Place 1 tbsp low-sodium tare in each warmed bowl. Add noodles. Ladle over hot birria broth and shredded meat. Top with mushrooms, bean sprouts, sliced radish and cilantro. Gently add peeled sous-vide egg. Finish with sesame seeds, spring onions and a squeeze of lime.\n\n**Why this works**\nSlow braising extracts collagen from chuck, producing a silky broth enriched with toasted chiles for deep, complex flavour. The tare concentrates umami (tamari, fish sauce) and balances acidity (vinegar) to season the broth just before serving. Sous-vide eggs cooked at 63 °C achieve custardy yolks that meld into the soup, echoing the richness of traditional ramen. Compliance refinement: the revised tare uses low-sodium tamari, less fish sauce, unsalted stock, and non-alcoholic acidity by default.\n\n**Plating / Pairing**\nServe in deep bowls with long-handled chopsticks and spoons. The broth should be glossy with a gentle chile sheen. Pair with a crisp Kölsch beer (limit to one glass) or a chilled hibiscus-lime agua fresca.\n\n**DGA Compliance Review**\nReviewed against the embedded DGA rule set. Recipe keeps its original flavor-map role while using whole/minimally processed ingredients, whole grains where applicable, controlled added sugar, protein diversity, and low-additive technique choices.\n- Made sake optional and non-alcoholic stock/vinegar the default.\n- Reduced sodium intensity by switching to low-sodium tamari and less fish sauce.",
