      "whyThisWorks": "Soaking brown rice softens the bran, reducing cooking time and yielding a creamy texture similar to arborio. Slowly adding hot stock and stirring agitates starch into the liquid, creating the classic risotto creaminess without cream. High-heat searing ensures scallops develop a Maillard crust while staying juicy. Compliance refinement: verjus provides wine-like acidity without alcohol while preserving risotto balance.",
      "plating": "Present risotto in a shallow bowl with scallops neatly perched. Garnish with micro-herbs. Pair with a minerally Italian white wine (Verdicchio) or sparkling water with lemon zest.",
      "fullMarkdown": "## Brown Rice Risotto alla Parmigiana with Artichokes & Scallops (Italian)\n**Servings: 4 | Active time: 50 min | Total time: 1 h 10 min (includes soaking)**\n\n**Mise en place**\n- Station A - Rice: Soak 300 g short-grain brown rice in water for at least 30 min to soften the bran; drain.\n- Station B - Stock: Heat 1.5 L vegetable or fish stock; keep warm on a back burner.\n- Station C - Vegetables: Mince 1 shallot, slice 200 g mushrooms, quarter 2 steamed artichoke hearts (200 g), grate 80 g Parmigiano-Reggiano.\n- Station D - Scallops: Pat dry 8 large sea scallops (320 g) and season with sea salt; bring a sauté pan to medium-high heat.\n\n**Ingredients**\n- 300 g short-grain brown rice (soaked), 1.5 L hot stock (vegetable/fish), 1 shallot, 2 tbsp (30 ml) olive oil, 200 g mixed mushrooms, 200 g cooked artichoke hearts, 80 ml verjus or 60 ml dry white wine as optional adult variation, 80 g Parmigiano-Reggiano, 20 g unsalted butter, 8 sea scallops (≈320 g total), 5 g fresh thyme, salt & black pepper.\n\n**Method**\n1. Build the soffritto: In a heavy saucepan over medium heat, sweat minced shallot in olive oil until translucent. Add drained brown rice and stir 2 min to coat the grains; this toasting promotes even absorption.\n2. Deglaze: Add verjus (or optional dry white wine); cook until mostly evaporated; if using verjus, reduce just until glossy. Ladle in hot stock (~200 ml at a time), stirring continuously. Maintain a gentle simmer and add more stock only when the previous addition is nearly absorbed. This process takes about 40 min; the rice should be creamy yet toothsome.\n3. Cook vegetables: In a separate pan, sauté mushrooms in a drizzle of olive oil until browned; season with thyme, salt and pepper. Add artichoke quarters and warm through.\n4. Finish risotto: When rice is just al dente, stir in sautéed mushrooms and artichokes. Remove from heat; vigorously beat in butter and grated Parmigiano-Reggiano to create emulsified creaminess. Season to taste.\n5. Sear scallops: Heat a non-stick or stainless pan until very hot. Add 1 tbsp oil, then scallops. Sear without moving for 2 min until a deep golden crust forms; flip and cook 1 min more. The centres should remain slightly translucent.\n6. Serve: Spoon risotto into warm shallow bowls; top with two scallops each. Drizzle with good olive oil and sprinkle with extra cheese and black pepper.\n\n**Why this works**\nSoaking brown rice softens the bran, reducing cooking time and yielding a creamy texture similar to arborio. Slowly adding hot stock and stirring agitates starch into the liquid, creating the classic risotto creaminess without cream. High-heat searing ensures scallops develop a Maillard crust while staying juicy. Compliance refinement: verjus provides wine-like acidity without alcohol while preserving risotto balance.\n\n**Plating / Pairing**\nPresent risotto in a shallow bowl with scallops neatly perched. Garnish with micro-herbs. Pair with a minerally Italian white wine (Verdicchio) or sparkling water with lemon zest.\n\n**DGA Compliance Review**\nReviewed against the embedded DGA rule set. Recipe keeps its original flavor-map role while using whole/minimally processed ingredients, whole grains where applicable, controlled added sugar, protein diversity, and low-additive technique choices.\n- Changed default deglazing liquid from wine to verjus, keeping wine optional.",
