      "whyThisWorks": "Whole-wheat pâte à choux still puffs from steam while adding fiber and nutty flavor. Banana/date-sweetened sorbet creates perceived sweetness without added sugar in the base; the only added sugar is the 16 g coating plus 20 g honey sauce, keeping the dessert at about 9 g added sugar per serving. Brushing and baking is the default compliance path; shallow-frying remains an occasional technique option.",
      "plating": "Serve immediately for hot/cold contrast. Pair with cinnamon cold brew, espresso, or sparkling water with orange peel; reserve fortified wine or mezcal for optional adult service only.",
      "fullMarkdown": "## Chocolate-Cinnamon Churro Sundae with Dark Chocolate Sorbet (Mexican)\n**Servings: 4 | Active time: 40 min | Total time: 2 h (includes chilling)**\n\n**Mise en place**\n- **Station A - Sorbet:** Blend 500 ml water, 80 g ripe banana purée, 60 g date paste, 55 g unsweetened cocoa powder, 100 g 85% dark chocolate, salt, and cinnamon; chill base 1 h.\n- **Station B - Churros:** Prepare whole-wheat pâte à choux with 125 ml water, 40 g butter, 5 g salt, 75 g whole-wheat flour, and 2 eggs. Set up piping bag with a star tip.\n- **Station C - Coating & sauce:** Mix 16 g raw sugar, cinnamon, and orange zest. Warm 20 g honey with butter and espresso; use mezcal only as an optional adult variation.\n\n**Ingredients**\n- **Sorbet:** 500 ml water, 80 g ripe banana purée, 60 g date paste, 55 g unsweetened cocoa powder, 100 g 85% dark chocolate, pinch salt, 2 g cinnamon.\n- **Churros:** 125 ml water, 40 g unsalted butter, 5 g salt, 75 g whole-wheat flour, 2 eggs, 20 ml high-oleic sunflower oil for brushing or 500 ml high-oleic sunflower oil for occasional shallow-frying.\n- **Coating:** 16 g raw sugar, 6 g cinnamon, 2 g orange zest.\n- **Caramel:** 20 g honey, 45 g butter, 20 ml espresso or mezcal only as optional adult variation, smoked sea salt.\n- **Garnish:** Toasted almonds, orange zest.\n\n**Method**\n1. **Make sorbet:** Blend water, banana purée, date paste, cocoa, chocolate, salt and cinnamon until smooth; chill 1 h, churn, and freeze until scoopable.\n2. **Prepare churro dough:** Bring water, butter and salt to a boil. Off heat, stir in flour; return to low heat for 1 min to dry. Beat in eggs one at a time until glossy.\n3. **Bake or shallow-fry churros:** Pipe 10 cm lengths. For default baking, brush lightly with oil and bake at 220 °C for 12-15 min until crisp; for occasional restaurant-style texture, shallow-fry at 180 °C until golden. Roll lightly in the 16 g cinnamon sugar mixture.\n4. **Make espresso honey butter:** Warm honey and butter until glossy; whisk in espresso and smoked salt. Use mezcal only as optional adult variation.\n5. **Assemble:** Place two mini churros in each bowl, add dark chocolate sorbet, drizzle with espresso honey butter, and garnish with toasted almonds and orange zest.\n\n**Why this works**\nWhole-wheat pâte à choux still puffs from steam while adding fiber and nutty flavor. Banana/date-sweetened sorbet creates perceived sweetness without added sugar in the base; the only added sugar is the 16 g coating plus 20 g honey sauce, keeping the dessert at about 9 g added sugar per serving. Brushing and baking is the default compliance path; shallow-frying remains an occasional technique option.\n\n**Plating / Pairing**\nServe immediately for hot/cold contrast. Pair with cinnamon cold brew, espresso, or sparkling water with orange peel; reserve fortified wine or mezcal for optional adult service only.\n\n**DGA Compliance Review**\nReviewed against the embedded DGA rule set. Recipe keeps its original flavor-map role while using whole/minimally processed ingredients, whole grains where applicable, controlled added sugar, protein diversity, and low-additive technique choices.\n- Reduced added sugar from >50 g/serving to about 9 g/serving.\n- Changed default cooking method to baked/air-fried with shallow-fry as occasional.\n- Replaced mezcal default with espresso.",
