      "whyThisWorks": "Dehydrating quinoa yields a crispy coating that echoes the crunch of tempura while providing whole-grain fibre. Cod’s high oil content prevents it from drying out during searing and roasting. Emulsifying lime juice and aji amarillo with egg yolks produces a tangy, spicy sauce reminiscent of acevichado without the raw fish.",
      "plating": "Plate with negative space for a refined look. Pair with a dry Riesling or a yuzu-sparkling water.",
      "fullMarkdown": "## Deconstructed Acevichado Cod with Quinoa Crust & Aji Emulsion (Nikkei)\n**Servings: 4 | Active time: 1 h | Total time: 1 h 30 min**\n\n**Mise en place**\n- Station A - Fish & crust: Portion 4 black cod fillets (150 g each); season with salt and refrigerate. Cook 100 g quinoa in 200 ml water until just tender; drain and dry on a towel. Crush 20 g nori sheets into flakes.\n- Station B - Pickles & garnish: Thinly slice 1 red onion and 1 cucumber; marinate in 30 ml rice vinegar, 5 ml honey and pinch salt. Dice 1 avocado; keep with lime juice.\n- Station C - Emulsion: Blend 2 egg yolks with 50 ml lime juice, 30 ml fish stock and 50 g aji amarillo paste; slowly emulsify 120 ml olive oil.\n\n**Ingredients**\n- 4 black cod fillets (≈150 g each), 100 g quinoa, 20 g nori, 1 egg white, 30 ml soy sauce.\n- Pickled onion & cucumber: 1 red onion, 1 cucumber, 30 ml rice vinegar, 5 ml honey, pinch salt.\n- Aji emulsion: 2 egg yolks, 50 ml lime juice, 30 ml fish stock, 50 g aji amarillo paste, 120 ml olive oil, salt.\n- Avocado puree: 1 ripe avocado, 10 ml lime juice, salt.\n- Garnish: micro-coriander, sesame seeds.\n\n**Method**\n1. Make quinoa crust: Spread cooked quinoa on a baking sheet and dehydrate in a 100 °C oven for 30 min until dry. Mix with crumbled nori and a pinch of salt. Brush cod fillets with beaten egg white and press onto quinoa mixture to form a crust.\n2. Sear fish: Heat a non-stick pan with 2 tbsp neutral oil. Place cod crust-side down and cook on medium heat until crust is golden and crisp (≈4 min). Transfer to a baking tray and finish in a 180 °C oven for 6-8 min until fish flakes easily.\n3. Make emulsion: In a blender, combine egg yolks, lime juice, fish stock and aji amarillo paste. With motor running, slowly drizzle in olive oil to create a thick emulsion. Season with salt.\n4. Assemble: Spread a swipe of avocado purée on each plate. Arrange cod fillet atop, crust side up. Scatter pickled onion and cucumber around, along with avocado cubes. Drizzle with aji emulsion and soy sauce drops. Sprinkle sesame seeds and micro-corinander.\n\n**Why this works**\nDehydrating quinoa yields a crispy coating that echoes the crunch of tempura while providing whole-grain fibre. Cod’s high oil content prevents it from drying out during searing and roasting. Emulsifying lime juice and aji amarillo with egg yolks produces a tangy, spicy sauce reminiscent of acevichado without the raw fish.\n\n**Plating / Pairing**\nPlate with negative space for a refined look. Pair with a dry Riesling or a yuzu-sparkling water.\n\n**DGA Compliance Review**\nReviewed against the embedded DGA rule set. Recipe keeps its original flavor-map role while using whole/minimally processed ingredients, whole grains where applicable, controlled added sugar, protein diversity, and low-additive technique choices.\n- Added DGA compliance metadata and sodium/additive-aware execution notes where relevant.",
