      "plating": "Serve with chopsticks. Pair with a chilled sake or green tea with jasmine.",
      "fullMarkdown": "## Miso-Glazed Salmon Bowl with Brown Rice & Sesame Kale (Japanese)\n**Servings: 4 | Active time: 35 min | Total time: 50 min**\n\n**Mise en place**\n- Station A - Rice: Cook 300 g brown rice in 600 ml water until tender; keep warm.\n- Station B - Salmon: Mix glaze: 35 g white miso, 12 g honey, 10 ml rice vinegar, 10 ml low-sodium soy sauce. Portion 4 salmon fillets (180 g each) and brush with glaze; marinate 15 min.\n- Station C - Kale & toppings: Destem 200 g kale; slice into ribbons. Julienne 1 cucumber, slice 1 avocado, cook 200 g edamame. Toast 20 g sesame seeds.\n\n**Ingredients**\n- 4 salmon fillets (≈720 g), 35 g white miso, 12 g honey, 10 ml rice vinegar, 10 ml low-sodium soy sauce, 300 g brown rice, 200 g kale, 1 cucumber, 1 avocado, 200 g edamame, 20 g sesame seeds, 30 ml rice vinegar, 5 ml sesame oil.\n\n**Method**\n1. Cook rice: Simmer brown rice until fluffy; season with a pinch of salt and 15 ml rice vinegar.\n2. Roast salmon: Preheat oven to 200 °C. Place glazed salmon on a parchment-lined tray; bake 10-12 min until just opaque. Broil for 1 min to caramelise the glaze.\n3. Sauté kale: Heat 5 ml sesame oil in a pan; sauté kale until just wilted; season with salt.\n4. Assemble bowls: Divide rice among bowls. Top with roasted salmon, sautéed kale, cucumber, avocado slices and edamame. Sprinkle with sesame seeds. Drizzle with remaining glaze diluted with 20 ml water as a sauce.\n\n**Why this works**\nMiso’s glutamates create deep umami; honey and mirin help caramelise the glaze. Brown rice offers chewiness and fibre. Quick sautéing retains kale’s nutrients, while edamame adds plant protein. Compliance refinement: rice vinegar replaces mirin and the honey amount is reduced while still supporting glaze browning.\n\n**Plating / Pairing**\nServe with chopsticks. Pair with a chilled sake or green tea with jasmine.\n\n**DGA Compliance Review**\nReviewed against the embedded DGA rule set. Recipe keeps its original flavor-map role while using whole/minimally processed ingredients, whole grains where applicable, controlled added sugar, protein diversity, and low-additive technique choices.\n- Reduced honey and replaced mirin with rice vinegar.\n- Changed soy sauce to low-sodium.",
