      "fullMarkdown": "## Sous-Vide Short Ribs with Purple Potato Purée & Sautéed Greens (American × Peruvian)\n**Servings: 4 | Active time: 1 h | Total time: 24 h (includes sous-vide)**\n\n**Mise en place**\n- Station A - Ribs: Season 1.2 kg beef short ribs with 20 g salt, 10 g black pepper, 4 g brown sugar or date paste and 20 g miso paste. Vacuum-seal with 2 crushed garlic cloves and 2 sprigs thyme. Preheat sous-vide bath to 68 °C.\n- Station B - Potatoes: Peel and cube 600 g purple potatoes; simmer in salted water until tender. Warm 100 ml whole milk and 50 g butter.\n- Station C - Greens: Wash and chiffonade 300 g Swiss chard or kale. Slice 2 garlic cloves. Toast 30 g chopped pecans for garnish.\n\n**Ingredients**\n- 1.2 kg beef short ribs, 20 g salt, 10 g pepper, 4 g brown sugar or date paste, 20 g miso paste, 2 garlic cloves, 2 thyme sprigs.\n- Purple potato purée: 600 g purple potatoes, 100 ml whole milk, 50 g butter, 20 g Parmesan, salt.\n- Sautéed greens: 300 g Swiss chard or kale, 2 garlic cloves, 15 ml olive oil, 30 g pecans.\n- Glaze: 40 ml unsweetened pomegranate reduction, 30 ml red wine, 10 ml balsamic vinegar.\n\n**Method**\n1. Sous-vide ribs: Cook sealed ribs at 68 °C for 24 h. After cooking, remove from bag, pat dry and sear in a hot pan until deep brown. Reduce bag juices with unsweetened pomegranate reduction, red wine and balsamic to glaze consistency.\n2. Make purée: Mash drained purple potatoes with warm milk, butter and Parmesan until smooth; season.\n3. Sauté greens: Heat olive oil; sauté garlic until fragrant. Add chard and cook until wilted; season and finish with toasted pecans.\n4. Serve: Spoon potato purée onto plates. Top with a rib portion, brush with glaze and arrange greens alongside.\n\n**Why this works**\nLong, low-temperature cooking converts collagen into gelatin without drying the meat, yielding fork-tender ribs. Miso and brown sugar in the cure enhance savoury depth and promote browning when seared. Purple potatoes provide antioxidants and a vivid colour contrast. Compliance refinement: the portion is positioned as an occasional red-meat dinner, with trimmed ribs, less added sugar, and an unsweetened pomegranate glaze.\n\n**Plating / Pairing**\nPlate with glaze drizzled around and garnish with micro-herbs. Pair with a bold Malbec or a tart pomegranate mocktail.\n\n**DGA Compliance Review**\nReviewed against the embedded DGA rule set. Recipe keeps its original flavor-map role while using whole/minimally processed ingredients, whole grains where applicable, controlled added sugar, protein diversity, and low-additive technique choices.\n- Reduced cure sugar from 10 g to 4 g total.\n- Changed glaze to unsweetened pomegranate reduction.\n- Flagged as occasional, portion-controlled red meat.",
