      "fullMarkdown": "## Sous-Vide Eggs (Technique / base preparation)\n**Servings: 4 | Active time: 5 min | Total time: 50 min**\n\n**Mise en place**\n- Set a sous-vide circulator to 63 °C in a deep pot or Cambro.\n- Prepare 4 cold large eggs, a slotted spoon, and an ice bath.\n- Choose the serving destination before cooking: ramen, bowls, Benedict variations, or toast.\n\n**Ingredients**\n- 4 large eggs\n- Water for sous-vide bath\n- Ice water for chilling if not serving immediately\n\n**Method**\n1. Preheat a sous-vide water bath to 63 °C.\n2. Lower eggs into the water and cook for 45 min.\n3. Transfer to ice water for 5 min if holding, or crack directly into a small bowl if serving immediately.\n4. Season only at service so salt does not toughen the tender whites.\n\n**Why this works**\nAt 63 °C, yolk proteins thicken into a jammy custard while whites remain tender. Precise temperature control prevents the chalky overcooking common with simmered eggs and adds protein without processed ingredients.\n\n**Plating / Pairing**\nUse as a finishing element for ramen, grain bowls, or Benedict-style plates. Pair with acid, herbs, and chile oil for contrast.\n\n**DGA Compliance Review**\nReviewed against the embedded DGA rule set. Recipe keeps its original flavor-map role while using whole/minimally processed ingredients, whole grains where applicable, controlled added sugar, protein diversity, and low-additive technique choices.\n- Added DGA compliance metadata and sodium/additive-aware execution notes where relevant.",
