      "fullMarkdown": "## Takoyaki-Inspired Omelette Roll (Japanese)\n**Servings: 4 | Active time: 20 min | Total time: 35 min**\n\n**Mise en place**\n- **Station A - Batter:** Beat 6 eggs with 30 ml dashi, 5 ml low-sodium soy sauce and 5 ml rice vinegar. Dice 100 g cooked octopus. Mix scallions, pickled ginger and 20 g toasted whole-wheat panko.\n- **Station B - Sauces:** Simmer tomato paste, tamari, rice vinegar, date paste, ginger and sesame oil into a glossy tomato-tamarind glaze. Mix Greek yoghurt with yuzu or lime and a spoon of homemade aioli.\n- **Station C - Toppings:** Prepare bonito flakes, aonori and sliced scallions.\n\n**Ingredients**\n- 6 eggs, 30 ml dashi, 5 ml low-sodium soy sauce, 5 ml rice vinegar, 100 g cooked octopus, 30 g scallions, 20 g pickled ginger, 20 g toasted whole-wheat panko or tenkasu made from whole-grain batter.\n- **Tomato-tamarind glaze:** 60 g tomato paste, 20 ml tamari, 15 ml rice vinegar, 10 g date paste, 5 g grated ginger, 5 ml toasted sesame oil.\n- **Yuzu-yoghurt aioli:** 70 g Greek yoghurt, 1 egg yolk or 20 g homemade olive-oil aioli, 10 g yuzu juice or lime, pinch salt.\n- Bonito flakes, aonori, sliced scallions.\n\n**Method**\n1. **Cook omelette:** Heat a rectangular tamagoyaki pan or non-stick skillet over medium heat. Lightly oil. Pour a thin layer of egg mixture to coat. Sprinkle with octopus, scallions, ginger and whole-wheat panko. Once set, roll omelette toward you. Repeat layering and rolling until all egg is used.\n2. **Glaze & slice:** Brush the roll with tomato-tamarind glaze and drizzle with yuzu-yoghurt aioli. Sprinkle with bonito flakes and aonori; the flakes will undulate from residual heat.\n3. **Serve:** Slice into rounds and serve warm.\n\n**Why this works**\nLayered tamagoyaki technique creates a tender, structured omelette. Octopus preserves the takoyaki reference; whole-wheat panko adds crunch. Replacing bottled sweet/salty condiments with tomato-tamarind glaze plus yuzu-yoghurt aioli removes common additive and added-sugar pitfalls while keeping sweet-savory umami.\n\n**Plating / Pairing**\nArrange slices neatly and garnish with extra scallions. Pair with cold green tea, roasted barley tea, or a light pilsner as optional adult service.\n\n**DGA Compliance Review**\nReviewed against the embedded DGA rule set. Recipe keeps its original flavor-map role while using whole/minimally processed ingredients, whole grains where applicable, controlled added sugar, protein diversity, and low-additive technique choices.\n- Replaced bottled sweet/salty condiments with homemade glaze and yuzu-yoghurt aioli.\n- Changed soy sauce to low-sodium.",
