      "fullMarkdown": "## Whole-Grain Gnocchi a la Huancaína with Grilled Prawns (Italian × Peruvian)\n**Servings: 4 | Active time: 1 h | Total time: 1 h 20 min**\n\n**Mise en place**\n- Station A - Gnocchi: Bake 600 g russet potatoes until fluffy; peel and rice while hot. Mix with 150 g whole-wheat flour, 50 g grated Parmesan, 1 egg yolk and 5 g salt. Roll into ropes and cut into 2 cm pieces; press with a fork.\n- Station B - Huancaína sauce: Blend 200 g queso fresco, 100 g aji amarillo paste, 200 ml evaporated milk, 20 g rolled oats or 30 g cooked potato (for body), 2 garlic cloves and 30 ml olive oil until smooth.\n- Station C - Prawns: Clean 12 large prawns (≈400 g); marinate with 10 ml olive oil, 5 ml lime juice, 1 g smoked paprika and salt.\n\n**Ingredients**\n- 600 g russet potatoes, 150 g whole-wheat flour, 50 g Parmesan, 1 egg yolk, 5 g salt.\n- Huancaína sauce: 200 g queso fresco, 100 g aji amarillo paste, 200 ml evaporated milk, 20 g rolled oats or 30 g cooked potato, 2 garlic cloves, 30 ml olive oil.\n- 12 large prawns, olive oil, lime juice, smoked paprika, salt.\n- Garnish: Chopped olives, boiled quail eggs (optional), parsley.\n\n**Method**\n1. Form gnocchi: Combine potatoes with flour, Parmesan, yolk and salt until a soft dough forms. Avoid overworking to keep light. Roll into ropes and cut. Dust with flour.\n2. Cook gnocchi: Boil salted water. Drop gnocchi in batches; cook until they float (≈2 min). Transfer to ice water briefly, then drain and toss with olive oil.\n3. Make sauce: Blend all sauce ingredients until smooth; adjust thickness with milk. Warm gently in a saucepan; do not boil.\n4. Grill prawns: Heat a grill pan; cook marinated prawns for 1 - 2 min per side until just opaque.\n5. Sauté gnocchi: In a skillet, heat 20 ml olive oil; sauté gnocchi until lightly golden. Toss with huancaína sauce. Top with grilled prawns, olives and parsley.\n\n**Why this works**\nBaking potatoes instead of boiling keeps them dry, producing lighter gnocchi. Using whole-wheat flour adds nuttiness and fibre without making the dumplings heavy. Huancaína sauce’s emulsified cheese and aji mixture coats each gnocchi, while grilling prawns adds smoky sweetness. Compliance refinement: rolled oats or cooked potato thicken the sauce without packaged crackers.\n\n**Plating / Pairing**\nServe in shallow bowls with sauce pooling around. Pair with a crisp Sauvignon Blanc or maracuya (passion fruit) juice.\n\n**DGA Compliance Review**\nReviewed against the embedded DGA rule set. Recipe keeps its original flavor-map role while using whole/minimally processed ingredients, whole grains where applicable, controlled added sugar, protein diversity, and low-additive technique choices.\n- Replaced packaged crackers with rolled oats or cooked potato.",
